The stakes are high – not only for people in Spain, but also for Europe. On November 29, the country holds a parliamentary election that could see the end of the Socialist-led government and potentially a coalition between the right and the far right, in one of the EU’s biggest and most influential member states. It could also signal the end of Pedro Sanchez’s third term in office. Among other things, the prime minister may pay the political price for two shocking train crashes and the migrant crisis in Spain’s North African exclave of Ceuta. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fb70dc0a-e434-11f1-8bdf-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/TALKING-EUROPE-SPAIN-DEBATE.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fb70dc0a-e434-11f1-8bdf-ebb478925cf0/w:1024/p:16x9/TALKING-EUROPE-SPAIN-DEBATE.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">64fa5a54-e1a9-11f1-b476-43a8f9a2efe8</guid><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 15:17:17 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Armen GEORGIAN</dc:creator></item><item><category>Reporters</category><title>Jamaica's beaches are increasingly off limits to locals</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/reporters/20261120-jamaica-beaches-off-limits-to-locals-property-developers-hotels</link><description>
Jamaica usually conjures up images of white sandy beaches, Rastafari culture and Bob Marley – but another reality exists. On this small Caribbean island, less than 1 percent of the coastline is freely accessible to local people. The rest has gradually been privatised, mostly for large hotel developments and private homes.  
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1952df8c-e41e-11f1-b8ec-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/Reporters-3.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1952df8c-e41e-11f1-b8ec-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/Reporters-3.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e9372ab0-e431-11f1-9387-ff4fa45f9015</guid><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 14:55:10 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Laurence CUVILLIER</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Cincinnati Zoo's famed Fiona the hippo dies days after eating a visitor’s hat</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261120-cincinnati-zoo-s-famed-fiona-the-hippo-dies-days-after-eating-a-visitor-s-hat</link><description>
Fiona, the hippo whose premature birth and fight for survival at the Cincinnati Zoo turned her into a global sensation nearly a decade ago, died Thursday from complications after swallowing a knit hat that was accidentally flung into her enclosure. She was 9 years old. “It is with extreme sadness that we share with you the devastating news that Fiona did not make it after multiple attempts from our amazing vets and keeper team to help her through what ended up being a medical emergency,” the zoo said in a statement.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f0ea273a-e4ef-11f1-a4bb-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261120-135316-135437-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f0ea273a-e4ef-11f1-a4bb-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261120-135316-135437-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f611b106-e4ef-11f1-a824-b3bef9076718</guid><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 14:48:52 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>REPLAY: Burnham, Von der Leyen hail 'turning point' in EU-UK relations</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261120-replay-burnham-von-der-leyen-hail-turning-point-in-eu-uk-relations</link><description>
British Prime Minister Andy Burnham hailed a "landmark" deal with the European Union on Friday, as the two sides sealed pacts on food exports, emissions-trading schemes and youth mobility at a summit in Brussels. EU chief Ursula von der Leyen welcomed a "turning point" in ties with Britain following Brexit. Watch our replay.
